## DESCRIPTION
## Inverse function theorem
## ENDDESCRIPTION

## DBsubject('Calculus - multivariable')
## DBchapter('Differentiation of multivariable functions')
## DBsection('Inverse function theorem')

########################################################################

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros("PGstandard.pl", "PGML.pl", "parserRadioButtons.pl");

TEXT(beginproblem());

$showPartialCorrectAnswers = 0;

@lst = ["\(dF_a\)", "\(DF(a)\)", "\( \partial_1 F(a) \cdots \partial_n F(a) \)"];

$mc1 = RadioButtons(@lst, "\(DF(a)\)");
$mc2 = RadioButtons(@lst, "\(dF_a\)");
$mc3 = RadioButtons(@lst, "\( \partial_1 F(a) \cdots \partial_n F(a) \)");

BEGIN_PGML


From an *algebraic* viewpoint, the inverse function theorem can be reformulated in several ways. Let [` A, B \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n `] be open subsets of [`\mathbb{R}^n`]. Fix [` a \in A `]. Let [` F : A \to B `] be a [`C^1`] function. Fill in the blanks so that the statements are equivalent to _[`F`] is a local diffeomorphism at [`a`]_.

1. ________ is an invertible [` n \times n `] matrix.

    [_]{$mc1}

2. ________ is an invertible linear map.

    [_]{$mc2}

3. ________ are a set of [`n`] linearly independent vectors in [`\mathbb{R}^n`].

    [_]{$mc3}
END_PGML

ENDDOCUMENT();